{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>The firstborn of his bull.</b> There is בְּכוֹר that is a term of greatness and kingship, as it is said ״אַף אָנִי בְּכוֹר אֶתְּנֵהוּ״ (Psalms 89), and likewise ״בְּנִי בְכֹרִי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 4) — the king who emerges from him, and that is Joshua, whose strength is hard as a bull's, to conquer many kings." "<b>His majesty is his.</b> Given to him, as it is said ״וְנָתַתָּה מֵהוֹדְךָ עָלָיו״ (Numbers 27)." "<b>And his horns are the horns of a re'em.</b> A bull — its strength is hard, but its horns are not fine; but a re'em — its horns are fine and its strength is not hard. He gave Joshua the strength of a bull and the beauty of the horns of a re'em (Sifrei)." "<b>The ends of the earth.</b> Is it possible that all thirty-one kings were from the land of Israel? Rather, there is no king or ruler who did not acquire for himself a palace and a holding in the land of Israel, which is esteemed by all of them, as it is said (Jeremiah 3) ״נַחֲלַת צְבִי צִבְאוֹת גּוֹיִם״ (cf. Sifrei)." "<b>And they are the myriads of Ephraim.</b> Those who were gored — they are the myriads that Joshua, who came from Ephraim, killed." "<b>And they are the thousands of Manasseh.</b> They are the thousands that Gideon killed of Midian, as it says (Judges 8): ״וְזֶבַח וְצַלְמֻנָּע בַּקַּרְקֹר…״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:51.641802311Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}